# ============================================================================
# WHAT BELONGS IN THIS FILE, AND WHAT DOES NOT
# ============================================================================
# One knob activates a box:  SERVER_PROFILE=<name>  ->  servers/server-<name>.env
#
# Scripts layer that profile UNDER this file:
#     docker compose --env-file servers/server-<name>.env --env-file .env
# Compose unions the keys and the LAST file wins, so anything ACTIVE here
# BEATS the profile. That is deliberate — it is how a host keeps a one-off
# override — but an uncommented copy of a profile's key silently defeats
# profile selection, which is the whole failure this layout exists to avoid.
#
# So every key a per-server profile owns — hardware, models, VRAM split,
# ports, which services run, role wiring — is COMMENTED OUT below. It stays
# visible as documentation, not as configuration.
#
# Keep ACTIVE here only:
#   SERVER_PROFILE        which box this is
#   GPU_<octet>_URL/KEY   the fleet block — identical on every machine
#   secrets               LITELLM_MASTER_KEY, provider keys, admin passwords
#   host paths            DATA_DIR / HF_HOME when the cache lives elsewhere
#
# ⚠️  A bare `docker compose` reads ONLY this file, so profile values fall back
# to compose defaults written for a different card. Use scripts/dc.sh:
#     scripts/dc.sh ps          scripts/dc.sh logs -f litellm
#
# GPU Inference Stack Configuration
# Copy this file to .env and customize for your server

# ============================================================================
# SERVER IDENTIFICATION
# ============================================================================
# SERVER_NAME=gpu-server-1
SERVER_PROFILE=default  # default, high-vram, multi-gpu

# ============================================================================
# GPU CONFIGURATION
# ============================================================================
# Which GPU devices to use (comma-separated, or "all")
# GPU_DEVICES=0
# Total VRAM available (in GB, for capacity planning)
# GPU_VRAM_TOTAL=96

# ============================================================================
# OLLAMA CONFIGURATION
# ============================================================================
# ENABLE_OLLAMA=true
# OLLAMA_PORT=11434
OLLAMA_HOST=0.0.0.0
# OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL=4
# OLLAMA_MAX_LOADED_MODELS=2
OLLAMA_FLASH_ATTENTION=1
# How long a model stays resident after its last request. Short by default:
# Ollama here serves only the low-volume gpu/chat/vision role, and this GPU is
# shared with vLLM and Infinity, so a pinned VL model is ~10 GB permanently
# unavailable to the chat model. Raise it if vision is hot on your box.
OLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE=5m
# Models to preload (space-separated)
# OLLAMA_PRELOAD_MODELS="qwen2.5:7b gemma3:12b"

# ============================================================================
# VLLM CONFIGURATION
# ============================================================================
# Which vLLM build this box needs is a property of its GPU. v0.23.0 predates
# Blackwell (sm_120) and does NOT degrade gracefully — every request dies — so
# an RTX PRO 4500 / 6000 box must set v0.25.1 or newer.
#   VLLM_IMAGE=vllm/vllm-openai:v0.25.1
#
# ── MODEL-SHAPE FLAGS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Each of these is OMITTED ENTIRELY when set empty, which is the only way to
# serve some models. Defaults reproduce the historical hardcoded behaviour.
#
#   VLLM_EXPERT_PARALLEL=     MoE-only. Empty for a DENSE model.
#   VLLM_TOOL_CALL_PARSER=    per-family (qwen3_xml, hermes, llama3_json, …).
#                             Empty also suppresses --enable-auto-tool-choice,
#                             since that flag requires a parser.
#   VLLM_REASONING_PARSER=    EMPTY FOR A NON-THINKING MODEL. Left at qwen3, an
#                             Instruct model returns content:null with the whole
#                             answer in reasoning_content — no closing </think>
#                             ever arrives — on every call. Consumers read
#                             .content, get nothing, and see an empty answer
#                             rather than an error.
#   VLLM_PREFIX_CACHING=      empty to disable
#   VLLM_CHUNKED_PREFILL=     empty to disable
#   VLLM_EXTRA_ARGS=          appended verbatim, for anything not modelled here
#
# ── Direct engine exposure ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   VLLM_BIND_ADDR / VLLM_ROUTER_BIND_ADDR
#     0.0.0.0    reachable from other hosts — needed only where a consumer
#                dials the engine port directly, bypassing the gateway
#     127.0.0.1  loopback: LiteLLM still reaches the engine over the compose
#                network, so gpu/chat/* is unaffected, but nothing off-box can
#                skip the gateway's auth, rate limits and spend tracking
#   The engine has NO auth of its own, so 127.0.0.1 is the better default for
#   a new box. 0.0.0.0 remains the shipped default only to avoid silently
#   cutting existing direct consumers.
#
# ── A second chat instance (needs VRAM for two models) ────────────────────
#   VLLM2_MODEL_NAME=<served name>   also puts it in the router's model map
#   VLLM2_HOST=vllm2:8000            required alongside it — the router's
#                                    upstream host for that instance
#   VLLM_KV_CACHE_DTYPE=fp8   halves the bytes per KV token, so the same VRAM
#                             holds ~2x the cache. Measured on an RTX PRO 4500
#                             (sm_120): 83,520 -> 173,856 tokens, concurrency at
#                             32k 2.55x -> 5.31x. Worth it whenever the workload
#                             is concurrency-bound rather than latency-bound.
#                             Empty = the model's own dtype.
#
# Example — a dense, non-thinking, tool-less model:
#   VLLM_EXPERT_PARALLEL=
#   VLLM_REASONING_PARSER=
#   VLLM_TOOL_CALL_PARSER=
# ENABLE_VLLM=true
# vLLM runtime image. Must be built for this box's compute capability —
# sm_120 (Blackwell) needs a CUDA 12.8+ build. Verify before deploying:
#   docker run --rm --gpus all --entrypoint python3 <image> \
#     -c "import torch; print(torch.cuda.get_arch_list())"
# VLLM_IMAGE=vllm/vllm-openai:v0.23.0
# Host port only (the container listens on 8000; LiteLLM reaches it as
# vllm:8000 over the compose network). Not 8000: that is deepdarshak-backend's
# API port on the same hosts.
# VLLM_PORT=8100
# VLLM_MODEL=Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct
# VLLM_MODEL_NAME=qwen2.5-7b-vllm
# Fraction of TOTAL device memory, and it budgets vLLM ONLY. Infinity and
# Ollama share this GPU and allocate OUTSIDE this fraction, so the sum
# (vLLM + Infinity ~4 GB + Ollama's vision model) must fit the card — vLLM
# cannot see the other two and will not warn you.
# Set it too low and vLLM refuses to start rather than degrading: it needs
# weights + activations + enough KV for ONE request at VLLM_MAX_MODEL_LEN
# (~4.5 GiB of KV for a 32k context on an 8B model).
# VLLM_GPU_MEMORY_UTILIZATION=0.65
# VLLM_MAX_MODEL_LEN=16384
# VLLM_MAX_NUM_SEQS=64
# The VALUE of each of these is the literal vLLM flag, not true/false: they are
# BooleanOptionalAction flags (each has a --no-enable-* twin, neither form
# takes a value) and compose cannot branch on a boolean.
#
# --enable-expert-parallel is MoE-ONLY and vLLM REFUSES TO START on a dense
# model with it set ("Number of experts in the model must be greater than 0").
# Serving a dense model (Qwen3-8B/14B/32B, Llama, Mistral) means setting:
#   VLLM_EXPERT_PARALLEL_FLAG=--no-enable-expert-parallel
# REMOVED — VLLM_PREFIX_CACHING_FLAG no longer exists. The three enable/disable
# toggles are now plain vars (VLLM_PREFIX_CACHING), where EMPTY omits
# the flag entirely instead of substituting a --no-* twin.
# REMOVED — VLLM_CHUNKED_PREFILL_FLAG no longer exists. The three enable/disable
# toggles are now plain vars (VLLM_CHUNKED_PREFILL), where EMPTY omits
# the flag entirely instead of substituting a --no-* twin.
# --no-, matching this file's own dense VLLM_MODEL above.
# REMOVED — VLLM_EXPERT_PARALLEL_FLAG no longer exists. The three enable/disable
# toggles are now plain vars (VLLM_EXPERT_PARALLEL), where EMPTY omits
# the flag entirely instead of substituting a --no-* twin.
# VLLM_TENSOR_PARALLEL_SIZE=1
# Parsers follow the WEIGHTS. Dense Qwen3 (8B/14B/32B) emits hermes-style tool
# calls; qwen3_xml is the Qwen3.6/Coder XML format and mis-parses them without
# erroring — the call is simply dropped. Set these whenever VLLM_MODEL changes.
# Structured-output decoding, as a JSON string (the only form vLLM's
# --structured-outputs-config accepts; per-key flags are rejected).
# disable_any_whitespace stops the degenerate case where a JSON grammar lets
# the model emit unbounded whitespace instead of the next key — it requires
# backend xgrammar or guidance, so the backend must be pinned alongside it.
VLLM_STRUCTURED_OUTPUTS_CONFIG='{"backend": "xgrammar", "disable_any_whitespace": true}'
# VLLM_TOOL_CALL_PARSER=hermes
# VLLM_REASONING_PARSER=qwen3
# Additional vLLM arguments — NOT WIRED UP either, for the same reason (a
# YAML-list `command` cannot word-split a string into separate argv entries;
# interpolating this would pass the whole string as ONE argument and vLLM
# would reject it). --enable-auto-tool-choice is already in the command list.
# VLLM_EXTRA_ARGS="--enable-auto-tool-choice --disable-log-requests"

# ============================================================================
# LLAMA.CPP CONFIGURATION  (alternative to vLLM — never both)
# ============================================================================
# MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE WITH vLLM: scripts/deploy.sh exits non-zero if both are
# true. No GPU in this fleet can host both engines at once.
#
# Reach for llama.cpp over vLLM when: the weights are GGUF and larger than
# VRAM (its -ncmoe/-ot CPU offload is mature), the box is pre-Hopper so FP8 /
# NVFP4 quants have no native kernel path (the A6000 is sm_86), or the model
# is qwen4exp/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next, whose host-memory PLE prefetch is still an
# open feature request in vLLM.
#
# EVERY setting below is passed as a LLAMA_ARG_* environment variable, not a
# command-line argument — llama-server exposes env vars for 138 flags. That
# is why this service needs none of the "the value IS the flag" contortions
# the vLLM section above documents.
# ENABLE_LLAMACPP=false
# LLAMACPP_PORT=8083
# Interface for the host-published port. LOOPBACK by default: llama-server has
# no auth and sets CORS '*', so exposing it on 0.0.0.0 hands anyone who can
# reach this box the model directly — bypassing LiteLLM's virtual keys, spend
# tracking and rate limits. LiteLLM does not use this port at all (it reaches
# the container as http://llamacpp:8080 on the compose network); it exists for
# local debugging and `scripts/benchmark.sh --direct`. Widen it only
# deliberately, and put a reverse proxy with auth in front if you do.
# LLAMACPP_BIND_ADDR=127.0.0.1

# Runtime image, pinned by BUILD NUMBER. The registry publishes images for
# only some builds, so most numbers do not resolve. See the long comment on
# the llamacpp service in docker-compose.yml for why b10884 here and b10644
# on the Blackwell/Flash-Next box (ggml-org/llama.cpp#28355).
# LLAMACPP_IMAGE=ghcr.io/ggml-org/llama.cpp:server-cuda-b10884

# Weights. LLAMACPP_MODEL_FILE is REQUIRED — compose refuses to render
# without it — and is a path relative to LLAMACPP_MODELS_DIR, mounted
# read-only at /models. Download instructions: docs/LLAMACPP.md
#   A6000 48GB : Qwen3-Next-80B-A3B-Instruct-UD-Q3_K_XL.gguf     33.19 GiB
#   Blackwell  : Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-UD-IQ3_XXS-00001-of-00003.gguf 76.3 GiB
LLAMACPP_MODELS_DIR=./data/llamacpp/models
# LLAMACPP_MODEL_FILE=Qwen3-Next-80B-A3B-Instruct-UD-Q3_K_XL.gguf
# LLAMACPP_MODEL_NAME=qwen3-next-80b

# ── Context ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CTX_SIZE is a POOL split across PARALLEL slots, so the per-slot context is
# CTX_SIZE/PARALLEL — and THAT is what the contract's >=32k applies to.
# Do NOT raise PARALLEL without raising CTX_SIZE to match.
#
# 163840/4 = 40960 per slot, deliberately ABOVE the 32768 floor. The floor is
# a floor on USABLE context, and llama-server charges prompt AND generation to
# the same slot — at exactly 32768 a real 32k prompt leaves no room to answer
# and is rejected: "request (33366 tokens) exceeds the available context size
# (32768 tokens)". 40960 leaves 8192 tokens of generation headroom.
#
# Leaving CTX_SIZE unset is unsafe: -fit defaults to `on` and may shrink an
# unset context all the way to FIT_CTX. Both are pinned here for that reason.
# scripts/health-check.sh asserts the live per-slot value, because these
# settings cannot catch a later edit that breaks the ratio.
# LLAMACPP_CTX_SIZE=163840
# LLAMACPP_PARALLEL=4
# LLAMACPP_FIT_CTX=163840
# Health-check floor ONLY — this is not passed to llama-server. The similarly
# named --kv-unified-per-slot flag is a CAP, not a floor, so setting it to a
# minimum can only reduce context; it is deliberately not wired up.
# LLAMACPP_CTX_PER_SLOT=32768

# ── Residency ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `off` is MANDATORY on a box without NVMe. The upstream default `auto` reads
# the rows of any tensor over 4 GiB from disk on demand instead of keeping it
# resident — seek-bound on the A6000's rotational SAS array.
# LLAMACPP_LAZY_MODE=off
# LLAMACPP_LOAD_MODE=mmap

# ── Fit ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Leave NCMOE and NGL EMPTY to let llama.cpp measure free VRAM at boot and
# offload only the experts that do not fit. This adapts when Infinity's
# footprint moves; a hardcoded -ncmoe goes stale and then fails closed with
# an OOM instead of degrading. Set both to pin startup exactly.
# FIT_TARGET is the MiB margin left free per device.
# LLAMACPP_FIT=on
# LLAMACPP_FIT_TARGET=2048
# LLAMACPP_NCMOE=
# LLAMACPP_NGL=

# ── Attention / KV ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# f16, not q8_0 — q8_0 KV asserts at load on the qwen4exp arch (PR #27742).
# LLAMACPP_FLASH_ATTN=on
# LLAMACPP_CACHE_TYPE_K=f16
# LLAMACPP_CACHE_TYPE_V=f16

# ── Throughput ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THREADS only matters if fit offloads experts to CPU.
# CACHE_REUSE is the analogue of vLLM's --enable-prefix-caching (default 0).
# LLAMACPP_THREADS=16
# LLAMACPP_CACHE_REUSE=256

# Extracts thought tags into `reasoning_content` rather than leaking <think>
# into message content — the equivalent of vLLM's --reasoning-parser qwen3.
# LLAMACPP_REASONING=auto

# 15 min: reading 33 GiB off a rotational array takes minutes before warmup.
# LLAMACPP_START_PERIOD=900s

# ── Per-model extras ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Both EMPTY for Qwen3-Next-80B-A3B. The Blackwell/Flash-Next profile sets
# OVERRIDE_TENSOR to put the 51B PLE table in host memory, and MMPROJ to add
# the vision tower.
# LLAMACPP_OVERRIDE_TENSOR=
# LLAMACPP_MMPROJ=

# Which GPU llama.cpp gets. Defaults to GPU_DEVICES; override only on a
# multi-GPU box that is deliberately co-tenanting the two engines.
# LLAMACPP_GPU_DEVICES=0

# ESCAPE HATCH. scripts/deploy.sh exits non-zero when ENABLE_VLLM and
# ENABLE_LLAMACPP are both true, because on a single GPU that does not fail
# cleanly — it OOMs mid-request or thrashes, reaching a consumer as
# intermittent 5xx rather than as a config error.
#
# Set to 1 ONLY on a box with more than one GPU where you have actually
# pinned the engines to different devices via GPU_DEVICES and
# LLAMACPP_GPU_DEVICES above. deploy.sh will then warn and echo both values
# so you can confirm they differ.
# ALLOW_VLLM_LLAMACPP_COTENANCY=0

# To bind the contract's chat roles to llama.cpp instead of vLLM, set these
# in the EMBER CONTRACT section below (or copy a servers/*.env profile that
# already does):
#   CONTRACT_INTERACTIVE_MODEL=openai/qwen3-next-80b
#   CONTRACT_INTERACTIVE_API_BASE=http://llamacpp:8080/v1
#   CONTRACT_BULK_MODEL=openai/qwen3-next-80b
#   CONTRACT_BULK_API_BASE=http://llamacpp:8080/v1
#   CONTRACT_FAST_MODEL=openai/qwen3-next-80b
#   CONTRACT_FAST_API_BASE=http://llamacpp:8080/v1



# ============================================================================
# THE FLEET  —  identical on every box, never edited per host
# ============================================================================
# Every GPU server's gateway, named by the LAST OCTET of its Netbird address.
# A box needs these to DELEGATE a role it cannot serve — a 32 GB card pointing
# gpu/chat/bulk at the 97 GB box — and the block is the same content on all
# four machines, so adding a fifth server is one edit copied everywhere rather
# than a per-host change.
#
# Named by ADDRESS, not by hardware or role: hardware gets replaced and roles
# move, but the address is what a peer actually dials. The port is part of the
# value because it differs per host: the five ddai boxes serve on 8090 (they
# share a base profile, and ddai3 cannot use 8080 — the platform's traefik
# holds it there), while dd4-skynet and crimson-llm2 serve on 8080.
#
# Keys are VIRTUAL keys minted on each peer, NEVER that peer's master key:
#   curl -X POST http://<peer>/key/generate \
#     -H "Authorization: Bearer $LITELLM_MASTER_KEY" \
#     -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
#     -d '{"key_alias":"peer-<thisbox>","models":["gpu/chat/interactive",
#          "gpu/chat/bulk","gpu/chat/fast","gpu/embed/bge-m3",
#          "gpu/rerank/bge-reranker-v2-m3"],"rpm_limit":600}'
#
# A profile refers to these by NAME, so no address is ever repeated in one:
#   CONTRACT_BULK_API_BASE=${GPU_85_URL}/v1      <- /v1 for chat and vision
#   CONTRACT_BULK_API_KEY=${GPU_85_KEY}
# The embed/rerank bases take NO /v1 — LiteLLM appends that path itself.

# dd4-skynet   — RTX A6000, 48 GB, sm_86 (Ampere)
GPU_40_URL=http://100.117.227.40:8080
GPU_40_KEY=

# ddai1        — RTX PRO 4500 Blackwell, 32 GB, sm_120
GPU_61_URL=http://100.117.227.61:8090
GPU_61_KEY=

# ddai2        — RTX PRO 4500 Blackwell, 32 GB, sm_120
GPU_62_URL=http://100.117.227.62:8090
GPU_62_KEY=

# ddai3        — RTX PRO 4500 Blackwell, 32 GB, sm_120
GPU_63_URL=http://100.117.227.63:8090
GPU_63_KEY=

# ddai4        — RTX PRO 4500 Blackwell, 32 GB, sm_120
GPU_64_URL=http://100.117.227.64:8090
GPU_64_KEY=

# ddai5        — RTX PRO 4500 Blackwell, 32 GB, sm_120
GPU_72_URL=http://100.117.227.72:8080
GPU_72_KEY=

# crimson-llm2 — RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell, 97 GB, sm_120
GPU_85_URL=http://100.117.227.85:8080
GPU_85_KEY=

# ============================================================================
# LITELLM CONFIGURATION
# ============================================================================
# LITELLM_PORT=8080
# LITELLM_ADMIN_PORT=8081
# Interface the gateway binds to. Consumers on other hosts need more than
# loopback, so pin this to the Netbird/LAN address rather than leaving it on
# every interface: LITELLM_BIND_ADDR=100.117.227.85
# LITELLM_BIND_ADDR=0.0.0.0
# LITELLM_DROP_PARAMS=true
# LITELLM_REQUEST_TIMEOUT=600
# Cache configuration
# LITELLM_CACHE_TYPE=redis
# LITELLM_REDIS_HOST=redis
# LITELLM_REDIS_PORT=6379
# Master key for LiteLLM admin access. CHANGE THIS — the committed value is a
# placeholder, and this gateway is reachable from other hosts.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Do NOT hand the master key to consumers. It is an admin credential: it can
# read spend, mutate models, and mint keys. Issue each consuming app its own
# virtual key off the Postgres this stack already runs, so each one gets its
# own spend tracking, rate limits, and independent revocation:
#   curl -X POST http://localhost:8080/key/generate \
#     -H "Authorization: Bearer $LITELLM_MASTER_KEY" \
#     -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
#     -d '{"key_alias":"ember-<product>","models":["gpu/chat/interactive",
#          "gpu/chat/bulk","gpu/chat/fast","gpu/chat/vision",
#          "gpu/embed/bge-m3","gpu/rerank/bge-reranker-v2-m3"],
#          "rpm_limit":600}'
# The returned sk-... goes in the consumer's GPU_<SERVER>_KEY, alongside a
# GPU_<SERVER>_URL of this stack's BARE ROOT — http://<this host>:8080, with no
# /v1 and no path (see README "How consumers address this stack").
LITELLM_MASTER_KEY=sk-1234567890abcdef

# Gateway log verbosity. EMPTY by default, deliberately.
#
# Setting this to --detailed_debug logs full request bodies. That measured
# +600ms on TTFT (917ms via the gateway vs 290ms direct, with identical
# per-token latency) and, with the MAX_LOG_SIZE/MAX_LOG_FILES limits below,
# can retain up to 500 MB of verbatim consumer prompts on disk. On shared
# inference serving several consumers, that is a data-retention decision, not
# a logging preference.
#
# Turn it on only while diagnosing, and truncate the logs afterwards.
#   LITELLM_DEBUG_FLAG=--detailed_debug
LITELLM_DEBUG_FLAG=

# ============================================================================
# EMBER CONTRACT  —  which model on this box fills each gpu/<task>/<role>
# ============================================================================
# These back the contract aliases at the top of config/litellm/config.yaml,
# the only names consumers (ember-ai and friends) are allowed to address.
# Leave them unset to accept the compose defaults: chat roles -> local vLLM,
# vision -> local Ollama, embed/rerank -> local Infinity.
#
# A role you cannot serve should be left UNSERVED rather than pointed at
# something that only half-works. Consumers check /v1/models at boot and
# route around a missing optional role (vision -> cloud); they fail loudly
# on a missing required one, which is the outcome you want.
#
# `bulk` defaults to the same deployment as `interactive`. Split them only if
# this box genuinely serves two chat models.
#
# CONTRACT_INTERACTIVE_MODEL=openai/qwen3.6
# CONTRACT_INTERACTIVE_API_BASE=http://vllm:8000/v1
# CONTRACT_BULK_MODEL=openai/qwen3.6
# CONTRACT_BULK_API_BASE=http://vllm:8000/v1
# CONTRACT_FAST_MODEL=openai/qwen3.6
# CONTRACT_FAST_API_BASE=http://vllm:8000/v1
# CONTRACT_VISION_MODEL=ollama/gemma3:12b
# CONTRACT_VISION_API_BASE=http://ollama:11434

# ============================================================================
# INFINITY  —  embeddings + reranking (the contract's embed/rerank roles)
# ============================================================================
# Serves BAAI/bge-m3 AND BAAI/bge-reranker-v2-m3 from one container.
# BOTH --model-id flags are mandatory: an embed-only Infinity returns 400 on
# /rerank, which degrades a consumer's retrieval to un-reranked order instead
# of failing visibly.
# ENABLE_INFINITY=true
# INFINITY_PORT=7997
# QUOTED, and it must stay quoted: scripts/deploy.sh and scripts/health-check.sh
# both `source` this file, and an unquoted multi-word value makes the shell run
# its second word as a command — `--model-id: command not found` — which under
# `set -e` aborts the deploy before it starts a single container. Compose's own
# dotenv parser strips the quotes, so the value reaching the container is
# unchanged either way.
# INFINITY_CMD="v2 --model-id BAAI/bge-m3 --model-id BAAI/bge-reranker-v2-m3 --port 7997"
# INFINITY_QUEUE_SIZE=512

# Vision model pulled into Ollama for the gpu/chat/vision role. Leave the role
# unserved (comment out CONTRACT_VISION_* above and remove the alias) on a box
# with no VRAM to spare for it. PROFILES set this, not .env.
# OLLAMA_VISION_MODEL=gemma3:12b

# ============================================================================
# SPEECH-TO-TEXT  —  the optional gpu/stt/whisper role (`stt` service)
# ============================================================================
# OFF by default. ENABLE_STT=true starts the service AND publishes the alias;
# otherwise LiteLLM lists it as unserved/stt, which no consumer addresses.
# PROFILES set these, not .env (check-env-overrides.sh flags them here).
#
# Measured on ddai3 2026-10-11 (9.6 s clip, large-v3-turbo):
#   cpu  int8, 24 threads   4.6 s, no VRAM
#   cuda int8_float16       ~1 s, 1.3 GB VRAM   (float16 needs ~2.1 GB)
# Do not swap in a smaller model for speed: small.en / distil-large-v3 heard
# "MMSI four one nine…" as "for 19001234".
# ENABLE_STT=false
# STT_DEVICE=cpu
# STT_COMPUTE_TYPE=int8
# STT_CPU_THREADS=24
# STT_MODEL=deepdml/faster-whisper-large-v3-turbo-ct2
# STT_PORT=8098
# Leave unset: derived from ENABLE_STT (lib-profile.sh stt_contract_alias).
# CONTRACT_STT_ALIAS=gpu/stt/whisper
# CONTRACT_STT_API_BASE=http://stt:8000/v1

# ============================================================================
# TEXT EMBEDDINGS (TEI) — optional, NOT the contract's embed backend
# ============================================================================
# Superseded by Infinity above for consumer traffic. TEI serves one model per
# container and has no reranker here, and its pooling/normalisation is not
# guaranteed byte-identical to Infinity's for the same weights — which for an
# already-built vector index is a silent-corruption risk, not a swap. Kept for
# non-contract use.
# ENABLE_EMBEDDINGS=false
# EMBEDDINGS_PORT=8082
# EMBEDDINGS_MODEL=BAAI/bge-m3
# EMBEDDINGS_MAX_BATCH_TOKENS=16384
EMBEDDINGS_MAX_CONCURRENT_REQUESTS=512

# ============================================================================
# MONITORING
# ============================================================================
# PROMETHEUS_PORT=9090
# GRAFANA_PORT=3000
GRAFANA_ADMIN_USER=admin
GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=admin  # CHANGE THIS!
# Exporter host ports. 9100 is a popular default — another node-exporter, a
# Cockpit, or an Authentik on the same box will already hold it, and a
# hardcoded port makes that a deploy failure with no knob to turn.
NODE_EXPORTER_PORT=9100
DCGM_EXPORTER_PORT=9400
REDIS_EXPORTER_PORT=9121

# ============================================================================
# NGINX REVERSE PROXY
# ============================================================================
# NGINX_PORT=80
# NGINX_HTTPS_PORT=443

# ============================================================================
# REDIS (for caching)
# ============================================================================
# REDIS_PORT=6390          # host side; loopback unless REDIS_BIND_ADDR is set. Not 6379: FalkorDB
# REDIS_BIND_ADDR=127.0.0.1
REDIS_MAXMEMORY=2gb
REDIS_MAXMEMORY_POLICY=allkeys-lru

# ============================================================================
# DATA PERSISTENCE
# ============================================================================
# Where to store model files, caches, etc.
DATA_DIR=./data
OLLAMA_MODELS_DIR=${DATA_DIR}/ollama/models
VLLM_CACHE_DIR=${DATA_DIR}/vllm/cache
HF_HOME=${DATA_DIR}/huggingface

# ── Shared model store (NAS) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where `make setup` (step 6/6) and `make model-store ARGS=sync` look for a
# checkpoint BEFORE going to Hugging Face, and where they publish one they had
# to download. Every box in the fleet serves the same public checkpoints, and
# Qwen3.6-35B-A3B in NVFP4 is 23.5 GB — 37 minutes over this site's ~13 MB/s
# internet link, against ~4 minutes at the ~92 MB/s the NAS actually delivers.
#
# The store is itself a valid HF_HOME (it has a hub/ with the same
# models--org--name layout), so nothing downstream needs to know it exists: a
# profile still names a plain repo id.
#
# Set it EMPTY to opt out entirely and always use Hugging Face. A box where the
# path simply is not mounted degrades to that on its own — the sync says so and
# carries on, because a missing NAS must make this slow, never broken.
MODEL_STORE_DIR=/media/NAS/shared-nas/ml_models/public_llm_models

# ============================================================================
# NETWORK CONFIGURATION
# ============================================================================
# Docker network name
# NETWORK_NAME=gpu-inference-net

# ============================================================================
# LOGGING
# ============================================================================
# LOG_LEVEL=INFO  # DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR
MAX_LOG_SIZE=100m
MAX_LOG_FILES=5

# Raw engine publishing defaults to loopback. Set a private interface explicitly
# only for authorized direct consumers; normal clients use LiteLLM.
# OLLAMA_BIND_ADDR=127.0.0.1
# EMBEDDINGS_BIND_ADDR=127.0.0.1
# INFINITY_BIND_ADDR=127.0.0.1
# VLLM_BIND_ADDR=127.0.0.1
# VLLM_ROUTER_BIND_ADDR=127.0.0.1
# nginx profile serves HTTP on port 80; TLS is handled by platform ingress.
# NGINX_BIND_ADDR=127.0.0.1
# Release updates: override LITELLM_BASE_IMAGE with an approved immutable digest,
# run offline checks and inference contract probes, then rebuild explicitly.
